# Drift Detection: Terminology & Architecture

Spec: DriftDetection
Purpose:
  Define terminology drift detection (when codebase terms diverge from
  project policy terms), architecture drift detection (when code violates
  declared architecture), violation lifecycle, accepted violations,
  and time-limited architecture exceptions.

Owns: lifecycle/drift.sdd

## Terminology Drift

  When the project policy defines preferred terms but the codebase uses
  different terms, this is terminology drift.

  Project policy:
    Use: Handler, Service, Repository

  Codebase:
    Manager, Processor, Provider

  AI creates:
    @observation.terminology-drift

  Format:
    @observation.term-O1

    type: terminology_drift
    policy_terms:
      - Handler
      - Service
      - Repository
    observed_terms:
      - Manager
      - Processor
      - Provider
    mapping:
      Manager → Handler
      Processor → Service
      Provider → Repository
    confidence: MEDIUM
    severity: LOW

  Rules:
    [TERM-01] Terminology drift is an OBSERVATION, not a finding.
              AI MUST NOT change code to match policy terms without human approval.
    [TERM-02] AI creates @observation.terminology-drift before any
              recommendation to rename.
    [TERM-03] Terminology drift observations are linked to the canonical
              @concept.* that the policy term maps to.

## Architecture Drift

  When the declared architecture in .sdd/ differs from the actual
  source code implementation.

  Declared (.sdd):
    @architecture.ddd
      Payment → Domain → Application → Infrastructure

  Observed (code):
    PaymentService → directly calls → PostgreSQL

  AI creates:
    @violation.V22

  Format:
    @violation.V22

    principle: @architecture.ddd
    found_in: payment/service.go
    reason: PaymentService directly accesses PostgreSQL, bypassing domain layer
    severity: HIGH
    status: DETECTED
    detected_at: 2026-09-01T14:30:00Z

  Drift Classification:
    VALID_DRIFT:       intentional and documented (has @exception.* or @decision.*)
    UNEXPLAINED_DRIFT: change without corresponding task/decision
    UNTRACEABLE_CHANGE: source changed with no task, decision, or workflow

  Rules:
    [DRIFT-01] Graph MUST detect all drift types (per graph/model.sdd).
    [DRIFT-02] Drift MUST be classified as VALID or UNEXPLAINED.
    [DRIFT-03] Untraceable changes MUST be flagged.
    [DRIFT-04] Drift MUST NOT be silently ignored.

## Violation Lifecycle

  DETECTED
    ↓
  REVIEWED
    ↓
  ┌──────────────┬──────────────┐
  ACCEPTED      FALSE_POSITIVE
    ↓              ↓
  FIXED          (rejected)
    ↓
  VERIFIED

  DETECTED:
    description: Violation found by drift detection or code analysis
    action: status set to DETECTED, notify reviewer

  REVIEWED:
    description: A human or automated gate has examined the violation
    action: status set to REVIEWED, outcome pending

  ACCEPTED:
    description: Violation is valid and should be fixed
    action: generate @task.* to fix, or @exception.* if intentional
    rule: [VIOL-01] Accepted violations MUST have a planned fix or exception.

  FALSE_POSITIVE:
    description: Violation is a false alarm
    action: mark FALSE_POSITIVE, archive

  FIXED:
    description: Code or architecture has been corrected
    action: status set to FIXED

  VERIFIED:
    description: Fix confirmed by re-running drift detection
    action: status set to VERIFIED, close violation

  Rules:
    [VIOL-02] Violations MUST be reviewed within 48 hours of detection.
    [VIOL-03] Unreviewed violations older than 7 days trigger escalation.
    [VIOL-04] FIXED violations MUST be re-verified by drift detection.

## Accepted Violations

  Sometimes a violation is knowingly accepted (for performance, legacy,
  or practical reasons).

  @violation.V22
    severity: HIGH
    status: ACCEPTED
    accepted_by: @decision.D91
    accepted_reason: "PaymentService is performance-critical; domain
                      layer adds unacceptable latency in benchmark tests"

  Rules:
    [ACPT-01] Accepted violations MUST reference a @decision.* record.
    [ACPT-02] AI NEVER flags an accepted violation as an error.
    [ACPT-03] Accepted violations are re-evaluated annually.

## Architecture Exceptions

  @exception.E22

  rule:
    @architecture.ddd (domain cannot access infrastructure)

  exception:
    RefundProviderAdapter

  reason:
    legacy integration with external provider

  expires:
    2026-12-01

  approved_by:
    @human

  status:
    ACTIVE

  Exception Lifecycle:
    ACTIVE → EXPIRED (on expiry date) → REVOKED (if revoked)

  Rules:
    [EXC-01] Every exception MUST have an expiry date (max 1 year).
    [EXC-02] AI MUST flag EXCEPTION_EXPIRED when expiry date passes.
    [EXC-03] Expired exceptions revert their violations to DETECTED status.
    [EXC-04] Exception extensions require a new @decision.* approval.

  Example expiry handling:
    On 2026-12-02 (day after expiry):
    @exception.E22  →  status: EXPIRED
    @violation.V22   →  status: DETECTED (was ACCEPTED via exception)
    AI alerts: "Architecture exception E22 has expired; violation V22
                is now active again. Extension requires new approval."

## Drift Integration

  Drift detection runs:
    - After task completion (per learning-gate.sdd)
    - Weekly (per health-score.sdd)
    - On demand (via sdd-health command with --drift flag)

  Detected drift flows:
    terminology_drift → @observation.terminology-drift → reviewed by compactor
    architecture_drift → @violation.* → violation lifecycle
    code_drift → @observation.* → candidate/knowledge pipeline

